  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe are to find an improved upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem constant C by finding a step function h:[0,2] -> [0,1] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum overlap integral. The current record is 0.38085, and our goal is to find a construction that shows C\u22640.3808. The previous program achieved a bound of 0.380904.\n\nInstead of using multi-stage hybrid-optimization methods as before, we will employ a two-phase approach:\n1. First use deterministic optimization based on a Fourier basis representation of the function to capture periodic behavior that likely helps in reducing overlap\n2. Then use a genetic algorithm with adaptive repair for the global optimization phase\n\nThe Fourier basis is chosen because the function needs to be smooth and periodic-like to minimize the maximum overlap. It helps spread the function in a way that avoids large overlaps at any particular shift.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: h(x) = a0/2 + sum_{k=1}^{K} [A_k*cos(2*pi*k*x/2) + B_k*sin(2*pi*k*x/2)]\n- Variables: coefficients a0, A_k, B_k\n- Constraints: \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1\n- Objective: minimize max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nThe Fourier coefficients will be optimized for K adaptive to the resolution. The Fourier function is then sampled over the interval [0,2] to obtain n_points samples.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Fourier initialization:\n   - Set maximum frequency range K_max = 40 (sufficiently large to avoid low-frequency oversmoothing)\n   - Analytically project the constraint \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1 onto the Fourier basis\n   - Solve for coefficients that satisfy the integral constraint (using normal equations)\n\n2. Fourier optimization:\n   - Use gradient descent to minimize -mean of exp(-alpha*(overlap_k - max_overlap)) (LSE approximation) for a large alpha (100) to focus on the maximum overlap\n\n3. GA optimization:\n   - Population-based search with repair function to maintain integral constraint\n   - Evaluate fitness via the overlap integral\n   - Tournament selection with cyclic crossover and adaptive mutation\n   - Allow K variation (frequency band expansion) during crossover\n   - Repair integral constraint by solving a least-squares problem\n\n4. Adaptive expansion of the frequency band:\n   - If improvement is not seen by a certain threshold, increase K by 5 and restart local optimization\n   - Balance resolution and frequency band size for time efficiency\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Initial K max frequency: 30\n- Penalties for integral constraint: 100.0 penalty coefficient for deviation from n/2\n- GA parameters:\n   - Population size: 200\n   - Tournament size: 5\n   - Crossover probability: 0.8\n   - Mutation probability: 0.1\n- Resolution n_points: transfer samples from parent resolution (150 samples for n=150)\n\nImportant variations to try:\n- Use different sampling methods: Fourier vs. random-scan optimization\n- Adapt penalty weights during optimization\n- Balance between Fourier optimization and GA by varying weights proportionally\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Primary GA phase: 600s\n- Four arithmetic sub-phases, each with 10 populations\n- Repair mechanism ensures integral constraint at each evaluation\n- n_points/dimensions: algorithm scales to n_points of up to 300 with current parameters\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- All candidates must satisfy 0<=h[i]<=1 and sum(h)==n_point/2 (verified before fitness evaluation)\n- final candidate has its overlap integral recalculated\n- Returns best found (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nParent validation:\n- If parent solution exists (from initial_h_values) then initialize Fourier optimization with parent coefficients\n- Otherwise initialize with uniform samples and Fourier solve\n\nThe Fourier basis ensures periodicity and smoothness advantageous for minimizing overlap.",
